ep by step.\n\nWe are given:\n\n- Demand: $ Q_d = 100 - P $\n- Supply: $ Q_s = 20 + 3P $\n- Tax: $ T = 8 $ dollars per unit\n\nWe want to find the **share of the per-unit tax borne by buyers**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find equilibrium price and quantity\n\nSet $ Q_d = Q_s $:\n\n$$\n100 - P = 20 + 3P\n$$\n\nSolve for $ P $:\n\n$$\n100 - P = 20 + 3P \\\\\n100 - 20 = 3P + P \\\\\n80 = 4P \\\\\nP = 20\n$$\n\nNow find equilibrium quantity:\n\n$$\nQ = 100 - P = 100 - 20 = 80\n$$\n\nSo at equilibrium, price is $ P = 20 $, quantity is 80.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the tax per unit\n\nThe tax per unit is $ T = 8 $.\n\nAt equilibrium, buyers pay this tax on top of the market price.\n\nMarket price at equilibrium: $ P = 20 $\n\nSo buyers pay an extra $ 8 $ per unit.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find how much of the tax is borne by buyers\n\nThe tax is split between buyers and sellers depending on the slope of the demand and supply curves.\n\nWe can find the **tax incidence** using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Fraction borne by buyers} = \\frac{MS'}{MS' + 1}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ M $ = marginal revenue\n- $ S $ = marginal cost\n\nBut a simpler way is to use the standard result:\n\n> The fraction of tax borne by buyers is:\n$$\n\\frac{P - MR}{P}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ P $ = market price\n- $ MR $ = marginal revenue\n\nFirst, find marginal revenue.\n\nFrom demand $ Q_d = 100 - P $, we differentiate with respect to price:\n\n$$\nQ_d = 100 - P \\Rightarrow \\frac{dQ_d}{dP} = -1\n$$\n\nSo marginal revenue $ MR = P \\cdot \\frac{dQ_d}{dP} = P \\cdot (-1) = -P $\n\nWait — this gives negative MR, which is not right. Let's recompute MR properly.\n\nFrom demand: $ Q_d = 100 - P $, so $ P = 100 - Q_d $\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to quantity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ_d}{dQ} = -1 \\Rightarrow dQ_d = -dQ\n$$\n\nRevenue $ R = P \\cdot Q $\n\nSo $ MR = \\frac{dR}{dQ} = P \\cdot 1 = P $\n\nWait — this is incorrect. Let's use standard method.\n\nGiven:\n- Demand: $ Q_d = 100 - P $\n- Supply: $ Q_s = 20 + 3